Observation:
Summary answer:
- Do the historical adjusted EBITDA values match between the IPO prospectus and the Annual Report? Answer: Yes — they are effectively the same for matching periods once units and rounding are accounted for, with only very small differences (generally 0–1 thousand or 0.1–0.3 million NOK) explained by table unit conversions, rounding/truncation and minor presentation differences between APM and segment tables. 

Key evidence (normalized to NOK million):
- 2019 Adjusted EBITDA: Prospectus = 307.5 (NOK million) / segment table 307.548 (NOK million). Annual Report = 307.549–307.941 (NOK million) depending on table; auditor-backed consolidated APM shows 307.549 (NOK million). Difference: <=0.392 million NOK (0.13%) — attributable to rounding/table presentation.
- 2020 Adjusted EBITDA (full year): Prospectus LTM to 30 June 2020 = 356.1 (NOK million) (LTM measure). Annual Report FY2020 Adjusted EBITDA = 390.841–390.842 (NOK million). These are different metrics (LTM to June 30 vs full year to Dec 31) so they are not expected to match. For six months ended 30 June 2020 the Prospectus reports 182.6 (NOK million) which equals the segment table 182.645 (NOK million). Annual Report does not present a six-month 30 June 2020 consolidated column — Annual Report numbers are FY. 

Explanations for apparent discrepancies:
- Units and table labels: Prospectus mixes “NOK million” narrative tables and “NOK 1,000” segment tables; Annual Report uses “Amounts in NOK 1000”. Interpreting units consistently removes most differences.
- Rounding/presentation: Prospectus explicitly warns that rounding may cause small differences between tables. Annual Report shows minor 1-unit differences between APM and segment tables (e.g., 390,841 vs 390,842) which are presentation/rounding differences.
- Different metrics/time windows: LTM vs FY numbers differ by construction; ensure you compare identical periods.
- Slight classification differences across tables (APM reconciliation vs segment aggregation) can cause small deviations but not materially different Adjusted EBITDA amounts.
- No evidence of accounting restatements in the provided excerpts that would materially change historical Adjusted EBITDA.

If you want, I can now produce a concise, normalized side-by-side table (all numbers converted to NOK million) for these periods — 2017, 2018, 2019, six months to 30 June 2019, six months to 30 June 2020, LTM to 30 June 2020, and FY2020 — showing the Prospectus value, the Annual Report value, absolute and percentage difference, and the document/table citation for each figure. Tell me which unit you prefer (NOK million or NOK 1,000) and I will produce that table.
